/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106589 #edb/ref 1061 136666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55ff9455-d9c5-5dfd-b9aa-7d7ad79f0ad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106590 #edb/ref 1061 136667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93903594-40c4-5f1f-b805-5569c124dd4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106591 #edb/ref 1061 136670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d73fd08-d0b3-549f-8356-26bd528a081c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106592 #edb/ref 1061 136671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66ba6652-6516-5c96-9fc2-f1a279bbcdce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106593 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106593 #edb/ref 1061 136674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f2b2f6c-0f00-518c-8edf-15031fffc70c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106594 #edb/ref 1061 136677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106594 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19ab5e9d-cd9a-5abe-b2d8-a8c2a4b0511a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1065 6654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8edf1574-04d8-5aea-839f-9c03c16e6ab5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107137 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1065 6688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1de6f70b-9797-56cc-b325-b5375efaf06b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107138 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1064 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1065 6738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "573fd3ba-e480-5a75-ba71-1fd15e977637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107139 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Pentagon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1064 "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1065 9114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "211863f5-73c2-57af-83e9-696d21971d5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107170 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification of the Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186114132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186114127 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1514e5e5-98af-5984-804b-835f796c778c" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
